WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:03.260
- [Narrator] Nothing
says Britain more than...

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:04.651
Nope.

00:00:04.651 --> 00:00:05.711
Nope.

00:00:05.711 --> 00:00:06.941
Keep going.

00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:08.599
Ah, yes, there it is.

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:10.232
A nice cup of tea.

00:00:10.232 --> 00:00:12.815
(bright music)

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:17.179
We know we have China to thank

00:00:17.179 --> 00:00:19.769
for introducing tea to the Western world.

00:00:19.769 --> 00:00:21.479
But how did it make its way to England

00:00:21.479 --> 00:00:23.969
and become the cultural
obsession it is today?

00:00:23.969 --> 00:00:27.529
Well, that's all thanks
to one Portuguese woman.

00:00:27.529 --> 00:00:29.579
The year, 1662.

00:00:29.579 --> 00:00:32.539
The person, Catherine of Braganza.

00:00:32.539 --> 00:00:35.609
She had just won the hand
of England's King Charles II

00:00:35.609 --> 00:00:37.718
with the help of a very large dowry

00:00:37.718 --> 00:00:41.369
including money, treasures, and spices.

00:00:41.369 --> 00:00:42.859
This worthwhile trade made her

00:00:42.859 --> 00:00:46.019
the Queen of England,
Scotland, and Ireland.

00:00:46.019 --> 00:00:47.943
When she arrived to her new homeland,

00:00:47.943 --> 00:00:50.047
she brought with her
packets of loose leaf tea

00:00:50.047 --> 00:00:54.361
in crates labeled Transporte
de Ervas Aromaticas.

00:00:54.361 --> 00:00:58.528
It's a theory that this was
later abbreviated to T-E-A, tea.

00:01:00.303 --> 00:01:02.092
Now tea could already be found in England

00:01:02.092 --> 00:01:04.810
but was only really used
for medicinal purposes.

00:01:04.810 --> 00:01:07.453
Catherine continued drinking
tea to her heart's content.

00:01:07.453 --> 00:01:08.286
- Mm.

00:01:08.286 --> 00:01:09.950
- [Narrator] And as the new
royal, everything about her,

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.842
including her beverage habits,

00:01:11.842 --> 00:01:13.773
was copied by other ladies desperate

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:16.553
to be just like their idol.

00:01:16.553 --> 00:01:18.293
Another thing Catherine
brought to the table

00:01:18.293 --> 00:01:22.483
from Portugal was the idea
of tea drinking experience.

00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:26.403
She popularized the use of
porcelain teacups and mugs.

00:01:26.403 --> 00:01:28.162
By the end of the 17th century,

00:01:28.162 --> 00:01:31.213
much of British aristocracy
were enjoying the hot beverage.

00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:32.235
- [Woman] Oo, delightful.

00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:34.616
- [Narrator] And soon
enough, so was everyone else.

00:01:34.616 --> 00:01:37.559
Today, while tea can be
found pretty much everywhere,

00:01:37.559 --> 00:01:40.343
it remains a special daily
pastime for the Brits.

00:01:40.343 --> 00:01:41.176
- Mm.

00:01:41.176 --> 00:01:45.343
- [Narrator] So carry on and
drink tea, people of England.

